<topic xml:id="pcp-installing"
 role="task" xml:lang="en"
 xmlns="http://docbook.org/ns/docbook" version="5.2"
 xmlns:its="http://www.w3.org/2005/11/its"
 xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude"
 xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink"
 xmlns:trans="http://docbook.org/ns/transclusion">
  <info>
    <title>Installing PCP</title>
    <meta name="maintainer" content="jsindelarova@suse.com" its:translate="no"/>
    <abstract>
      <para>
        This topic covers installing the PCP packages.
      </para>
    </abstract>
  </info>
  <section xml:id="pcp-installing-standard">
    <title>Standard mode</title>
    <para>
      Install the <package>pcp</package> package with &zypper;:
    </para>
<screen>&prompt.sudo;<command>zypper install pcp</command></screen>
    <para>
      Alternatively, install the <package>pcp-zeroconf</package> package
      instead. It pulls in <package>pcp</package> as a dependency and
      additionally enables and starts <command>pmcd</command>,
      <command>pmlogger</command>, and <command>pmproxy</command> automatically,
      with a shorter, 10-second sampling interval. Use it if you want local
      monitoring running immediately without configuring the services yourself,
      as described in <xref linkend="pcp-managing-services"/>:
    </para>
<screen>&prompt.sudo;<command>zypper install pcp-zeroconf</command></screen>
  </section>
  <section xml:id="pcp-installing-immutable">
    <title>Immutable mode</title>
    <para>
      On the immutable mode of &productnameshort;, install either package
      through &tr-up; instead:
    </para>
<screen>&prompt.sudo;<command>transactional-update pkg install pcp</command></screen>
    <para>
      Or, for the zero-configuration variant:
    </para>
<screen>&prompt.sudo;<command>transactional-update pkg install pcp-zeroconf</command></screen>
    <para>
      Changes take effect only after a reboot:
    </para>
<screen>&prompt.sudo;<command>reboot</command></screen>
  </section>
</topic>
